{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And you shall count for yourself seven sabbaths of years.</b> The reason for the Jubilee is like the reason for the Sabbatical year, and in addition to this there are yet other matters within it — for when it says 'and you shall count for yourself,' hear from this that there is a comparison and a correlation of these years with the number of the days of a person's life, which for the most part come to the number fifty. For the days of our years in them are seventy years, and the first twenty years are not included in this, because since he is not yet subject to punishment in the heavenly court, therefore all his deeds during that time are reckoned as nothing, because 'a man is born a wild ass's colt' (Job 11:12), and the light of his intellect is not yet complete to understand from the concepts so that he will not turn his steps from the path of the upright. And from there onward he begins to count, so that he may know how much remains to him in the years in which 'he who gets wisdom buys understanding,' and the medicine of life will be his alone, and strangers shall have no share with him. Therefore it says 'and you shall count for yourself' etc. — if you count for yourself, for your own good and your own benefit, in order to spend all your days on spiritual matters, then 'and the days of the seven sabbaths of years shall be yours' — that is to say, they shall be yours alone and not for strangers with you. But if you squander your days in vanity to gather and amass, then the seed shall not be yours, for you will leave to others the wealth and the wall that you built and that you planted. For for the most part a person cannot hold onto his own property except for this number of years. But the fiftieth year from the twentieth year — which is the seventieth year from the day of his birth — then all are equal in wealth and property, and he will no longer harvest his harvest, nor the grapes of his nazirite-wine that he set apart and hid on the day of acquisition, and his hands refused to touch them, and his eye was grudging in giving them to any of his brethren — and if he did not even give to himself, how much the more so did he not let others enjoy them. Behold, at that time 'the increase of his house shall depart, flowing away in the day of his wrath' (ibid. 20:28), and everything is ownerless and all are equal in it, rich man and wayfarer alike, and it is a time of proclaiming liberty, and a slave is free of his master." "And in order that this matter may be a remembrance between a person's eyes — that wealth does not satisfy — HaShem commanded to sanctify the fiftieth year: it shall not be sown nor worked. For just as He commanded to sanctify the seventh year, to know and to make known that 'to HaShem belongs the earth,' so too He commanded to sanctify the fiftieth year, to make known that there is a boundary and a limit to all his doings, and that he is not the owner of the field absolutely, but is rather like a sojourner in the land, and he has no power over it except for fifty years. Therefore 'the land shall not be sold in perpetuity,' for Elohim did not grant him authority to sell something that is not his. And this is why He gave a reason and said: 'כִּי לִי הָאָרֶץ כִּי גֵרִים וְתוֹשָׁבִים אַתֶּם עִמָּדִי' — from the juxtaposition of 'you are with Me' to 'sojourners,' hear from this that it is specifically about the world to come, for then you are with Me — there you are permanent residents; but in this world you are sojourners. And the Holy One, blessed be He, gave you the land by way of tenancy or by way of loan — and how can you sell something that is not yours?" "And the matter of sounding the shofar and proclaiming liberty, and on the Day of Atonement specifically, is like the day of the giving of the Torah, on which Israel went forth to freedom from the evil inclination and from suffering. And likewise the Day of Atonement is a time of freedom from the hand of an old and foolish king, an enemy and a vengeful one — he is the adversary, he is the evil inclination. And all this is to remind a person of the day of freedom among the dead who are free, and that the time of freedom and liberty should always be before his eyes. And thus wrote Maharil, may his memory be for a blessing. And all this is by way of allusion, in which most of the commentators of our Torah have walked. But according to the plain sense, the Sabbatical year and the Jubilee have one reason for both of them, and it is so that we may place our trust in HaShem and know that 'to HaShem belongs the earth.' Therefore it says concerning this Jubilee year: 'מִן הַשָּׂדֶה תֹּאכְלוּ אֶת תְּבוּאָתָהּ' — you shall not hold the field as your field, but merely as a field that is not yours."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The abbreviation מַהֲרִי״א ע״ש is resolved as Maharil (Rabbi Yaakov Levi, after whom the Maharil is named) with the honorific עִשֵּׂה שָׁלוֹם; the attribution is inferred from context and common citation practice in Kli Yakar."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:36.287247934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}